Step 1: The Imports (Gathering the Tools)

    Before we build anything, we need to bring in our toolboxes.

    Python:

        import discord
        import requests
        import os
        from dotenv import load_dotenv

    import discord: This brings in the official Discord library. It contains all the blueprints for
                    connecting to Discord's servers, reading messages, and typing in channels.

    import requests: This is our "Courier." It is the library we use to send data packages
                      over the internet to your Render API.

    import os: This stands for Operating System. It allows our Python script to look inside your
               computer's folders and read hidden files.

    from dotenv import load_dotenv: This is our lockpick. It allows Python to open and read your hidden .env file
                                    where your passwords are kept.

Step 2: Configuration & Security

    Python:

        load_dotenv()
        TOKEN = os.getenv("DISCORD_TOKEN")
        API_URL = "https://your-render-app-name.onrender.com/chat"

    load_dotenv(): This is a function call. It tells Python, "Look in this folder for a file named .env and
                   load all the secrets inside it into memory."

    os.getenv("DISCORD_TOKEN"): This is a method. We are asking the OS to get the environment variable named DISCORD_TOKEN.
                                We save that password into the TOKEN variable.

    API_URL: This is just a string variable. It is the exact GPS coordinate of your Render "Kitchen."

Step 3: Building the Bot's Body & Senses (Classes)

    Discord values privacy. If you just drop a bot into a server, it is blind and deaf by default.
    We have to explicitly give it "Intents" (senses).

    Python:

        intents = discord.Intents.default()
        intents.message_content = True
        client = discord.Client(intents=intents)

    discord.Intents.default(): Intents is a Class (a blueprint). We are asking Discord for the default set of permissions
                               (like seeing when people log in or out).

    intents.message_content = True: This is crucial. We are modifying a property of the Intents class. We are explicitly
                                    turning ON the bot's ability to read the actual text people type.

    discord.Client(intents=intents): Client is a massive Class. Think of Client as the physical, robotic body of your bot.
                                     We are building this robot body and plugging the intents (senses) we just defined into
                                     its head. We name this robot client.

